WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.312
Welcome to Asylum.

2
00:00:01.312 --> 00:00:05.111
We will start with the five-year-old
version, then build the real

3
00:00:05.111 --> 00:00:06.747
workflow one step at a time.

4
00:00:06.747 --> 00:00:10.020
You do not need to memorize every screen,
and you do not

5
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.422
need to be a Git expert.

6
00:00:11.422 --> 00:00:14.461
By the end, you will know what to open,
what to ask,

7
00:00:14.461 --> 00:00:17.617
what to watch, how to check the result,
and when it is

8
00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:18.617
safe to keep it.

9
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:23.137
Imagine you ask three people to build the
same little bridge.

10
00:00:23.137 --> 00:00:26.721
If all three reach into one pile of
blocks, their hands collide

11
00:00:26.721 --> 00:00:28.655
and nobody knows who changed what.

12
00:00:28.655 --> 00:00:31.954
So you give each person their own table,
their own copy of

13
00:00:31.954 --> 00:00:34.343
the blocks, and the same instruction card.

14
00:00:34.343 --> 00:00:38.268
When they finish, you compare the bridges
and keep the strongest one.

15
00:00:38.268 --> 00:00:41.339
Asylum does that with coding agents and
project files.

16
00:00:42.389 --> 00:00:44.479
Asylum uses three important words.

17
00:00:44.479 --> 00:00:48.104
A project is the box of building blocks,
which is your code

18
00:00:48.104 --> 00:00:49.104
repository.

19
00:00:49.104 --> 00:00:52.852
A task is the instruction card, written in
ordinary language.

20
00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:55.740
A run is one agent's attempt, at its own
table.

21
00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:59.549
Give one task to three agents and you get
three separate runs.

22
00:00:59.549 --> 00:01:03.175
If you remember project, task, and run,
the rest of the app

23
00:01:03.175 --> 00:01:04.526
has somewhere to land.

24
00:01:05.576 --> 00:01:07.378
The whole product is one loop.

25
00:01:07.378 --> 00:01:09.599
Open a project. Describe one outcome.

26
00:01:09.599 --> 00:01:13.382
Let one or more agents work in isolation.
Compare the evidence.

27
00:01:13.382 --> 00:01:17.165
Then merge the winner, or send feedback
and let a promising run

28
00:01:17.165 --> 00:01:18.165
try again.

29
00:01:18.165 --> 00:01:22.128
Everything else in Asylum helps you see
that loop clearly, make it

30
00:01:22.128 --> 00:01:24.350
safer, or repeat it without busywork.

31
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.881
If this is your first coding tool, follow
the blue path and

32
00:01:28.881 --> 00:01:30.888
use one agent for your first task.

33
00:01:30.888 --> 00:01:35.077
Asylum explains branches and worktrees in
tooltips, and every important

34
00:01:35.077 --> 00:01:37.142
change waits for your confirmation.

35
00:01:37.142 --> 00:01:41.214
If you already know Git, the exact branch,
worktree, terminal output,

36
00:01:41.214 --> 00:01:43.161
checks, and diff are all visible.

37
00:01:43.161 --> 00:01:47.350
Beginners and experienced developers use
the same workflow at different

38
00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:48.350
depths.

39
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.617
Install the packaged app for your
operating system, make sure Git is

40
00:01:53.617 --> 00:01:58.020
available, and install at least one
supported coding-agent command-line

41
00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.315
tool that you can already sign in to.

42
00:02:00.315 --> 00:02:04.408
On first launch, Asylum's setup doctor
checks the repository, base

43
00:02:04.408 --> 00:02:08.067
branch, worktree support, agent
executables, authentication

44
00:02:08.067 --> 00:02:10.547
verification, and the project toolchain.

45
00:02:10.547 --> 00:02:12.904
Installed means the command was found.

46
00:02:12.904 --> 00:02:15.322
Verified means it completed a real run.

47
00:02:16.372 --> 00:02:19.339
Click Open project and choose a code
repository.

48
00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:21.935
Opening it does not modify the repository.

49
00:02:21.935 --> 00:02:26.199
If you choose a plain folder, Asylum asks
before initializing Git and

50
00:02:26.199 --> 00:02:28.362
creates only an empty first commit.

51
00:02:28.362 --> 00:02:32.441
A repository can declare setup commands
and environment values, so

52
00:02:32.441 --> 00:02:36.335
Asylum withholds those until you
explicitly trust that project.

53
00:02:36.335 --> 00:02:40.043
Trust can be revoked, and checks are gated
by the same rule.

54
00:02:41.093 --> 00:02:43.212
The Tasks board is the home screen.

55
00:02:43.212 --> 00:02:46.058
The left side keeps projects and tasks in
view.

56
00:02:46.058 --> 00:02:49.389
The composer at the top is where you
describe new work.

57
00:02:49.389 --> 00:02:53.325
Below it, run cards show every attempt
without hiding its branch,

58
00:02:53.325 --> 00:02:56.353
private worktree, output, checks, or
elapsed time.

59
00:02:56.353 --> 00:02:58.169
Review is the next major stop.

60
00:02:58.169 --> 00:03:02.408
Search, Notes, and the other tools stay
available without interrupting

61
00:03:02.408 --> 00:03:03.408
the task.

62
00:03:04.458 --> 00:03:07.162
A strong first task is small and testable.

63
00:03:07.162 --> 00:03:11.668
Say what should be true, name any
constraint that matters, and include

64
00:03:11.668 --> 00:03:12.956
an acceptance check.

65
00:03:12.956 --> 00:03:17.334
For example: replace generic validation
failures with field-specific

66
00:03:17.334 --> 00:03:21.840
messages, add tests for empty names and
malformed email addresses, and

67
00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.579
keep the public API stable.

68
00:03:23.579 --> 00:03:28.021
The built-in templates help shape a bug
fix, feature, test, refactor,

69
00:03:28.021 --> 00:03:29.695
review, or design request.

70
00:03:30.745 --> 00:03:33.691
Start with one ready agent while learning
the loop.

71
00:03:33.691 --> 00:03:36.984
Use two or more when independent
approaches are valuable.

72
00:03:36.984 --> 00:03:41.086
The duel, triad, and swarm layouts are
reusable agent selections; swarm

73
00:03:41.086 --> 00:03:43.108
also limits how many start at once.

74
00:03:43.108 --> 00:03:46.747
Your global parallel limit protects the
machine and your quota.

75
00:03:46.747 --> 00:03:50.733
Extra runs wait in the queue and start as
capacity becomes available.

76
00:03:50.733 --> 00:03:54.315
More agents are useful only when
comparison is worth the cost.

77
00:03:55.365 --> 00:03:58.489
Under the hood, each run gets a branch and
a Git worktree.

78
00:03:58.489 --> 00:04:00.427
A branch is a named line of changes.

79
00:04:00.427 --> 00:04:03.928
A worktree is another checkout of the same
history in a different

80
00:04:03.928 --> 00:04:06.998
folder. That is the separate table from
our bridge story.

81
00:04:06.998 --> 00:04:10.283
Agents can edit the same source file at
the same time because

82
00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:11.899
each one touches its own copy.

83
00:04:11.899 --> 00:04:15.561
Asylum creates the worktrees, tracks them,
and cleans them up safely

84
00:04:15.561 --> 00:04:16.561
later.

85
00:04:17.611 --> 00:04:21.692
A run's status says what happened to the
process: queued, running,

86
00:04:21.692 --> 00:04:23.670
succeeded, failed, or cancelled.

87
00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:28.122
Activity says what the agent is doing
right now: working, blocked, done,

88
00:04:28.122 --> 00:04:31.461
or idle. The orange blocked chip is the
one to notice.

89
00:04:31.461 --> 00:04:35.109
It means the agent is waiting for you, so
open its terminal

90
00:04:35.109 --> 00:04:38.819
and answer. Succeeded only means the
command exited cleanly.

91
00:04:38.819 --> 00:04:41.292
It does not prove the change is correct.

92
00:04:42.342 --> 00:04:45.608
Open a run's terminal when you need the
full conversation.

93
00:04:45.608 --> 00:04:47.466
Cancel if the direction is wrong.

94
00:04:47.466 --> 00:04:49.268
Retry after a temporary failure.

95
00:04:49.268 --> 00:04:52.872
A follow-up or review comment continues in
the same worktree and

96
00:04:52.872 --> 00:04:56.758
increments the attempt count, so the agent
keeps its files instead of

97
00:04:56.758 --> 00:04:57.758
starting over.

98
00:04:57.758 --> 00:05:01.587
If Asylum closes during a run, the old
live terminal cannot survive,

99
00:05:01.587 --> 00:05:05.472
but the worktree and saved transcript
remain available for inspection

100
00:05:05.472 --> 00:05:06.472
and retry.

101
00:05:07.522 --> 00:05:09.137
Select a run and open Review.

102
00:05:09.137 --> 00:05:11.809
Switch between runs with the comparison
buttons.

103
00:05:11.809 --> 00:05:15.539
Green lines were added, red lines were
removed, and unchanged lines

104
00:05:15.539 --> 00:05:16.539
provide context.

105
00:05:16.539 --> 00:05:20.214
Unified and side-by-side views show the
same evidence in different

106
00:05:20.214 --> 00:05:21.214
shapes.

107
00:05:21.214 --> 00:05:25.055
Ask whether the right files changed,
whether the request is complete,

108
00:05:25.055 --> 00:05:26.892
and whether unrelated code moved.

109
00:05:26.892 --> 00:05:30.233
A confident summary is useful, but the
diff is the source of

110
00:05:30.233 --> 00:05:31.233
truth.

111
00:05:32.283 --> 00:05:34.611
Run checks for each serious candidate.

112
00:05:34.611 --> 00:05:38.838
Asylum detects the project's real type
check, lint, and test commands

113
00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:43.065
for Rust, JavaScript, Python, and Go, and
runs them inside that run's

114
00:05:43.065 --> 00:05:45.822
worktree. A passing badge is strong
evidence.

115
00:05:45.822 --> 00:05:49.742
A failing or active check blocks merge and
pull-request actions.

116
00:05:49.742 --> 00:05:52.867
If you do not read code yet, use checks as
a health

117
00:05:52.867 --> 00:05:57.216
signal, then ask for an explanation when
something still feels unclear.

118
00:05:58.266 --> 00:06:01.580
If a run is close, click the exact changed
line and write

119
00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:03.498
the correction in plain language.

120
00:06:03.498 --> 00:06:06.347
The comment is stored against that file
and line.

121
00:06:06.347 --> 00:06:10.183
Send the open comments back to the agent
and Asylum starts another

122
00:06:10.183 --> 00:06:11.869
attempt in the same worktree.

123
00:06:11.869 --> 00:06:14.950
This is often better than discarding a
good approach.

124
00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:19.019
Review becomes a loop: inspect, comment,
revise, and check again until

125
00:06:19.019 --> 00:06:21.054
one candidate is clearly strongest.

126
00:06:22.104 --> 00:06:26.133
A successful run stays uncommitted so you
can stage only the hunks

127
00:06:26.133 --> 00:06:27.231
or files you want.

128
00:06:27.231 --> 00:06:31.260
Before merge, Asylum blocks failed checks,
verifies that your base

129
00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:35.472
worktree is clean, and performs a
non-destructive conflict preflight.

130
00:06:35.472 --> 00:06:37.303
Then it asks for confirmation.

131
00:06:37.303 --> 00:06:41.271
Choose a regular merge, a squash merge, or
create a pull request.

132
00:06:41.271 --> 00:06:45.116
Cleanup removes only clean finished
worktrees and safely merged

133
00:06:45.116 --> 00:06:47.863
branches. Losing or dirty work is left
alone.

134
00:06:48.913 --> 00:06:51.156
Notes are plain Markdown project memory.

135
00:06:51.156 --> 00:06:55.024
Keep them private or store them in the
repository, add properties and

136
00:06:55.024 --> 00:06:58.219
tags, link notes with double brackets, and
see backlinks.

137
00:06:58.219 --> 00:07:01.303
Attach a note to a task or run and its
Markdown becomes

138
00:07:01.303 --> 00:07:02.303
agent context.

139
00:07:02.303 --> 00:07:05.722
This is the right place for a
specification, a decision, or a

140
00:07:05.722 --> 00:07:08.637
recurring constraint that should outlive
one prompt.

141
00:07:08.637 --> 00:07:12.394
The code and the knowledge stay connected
without hiding the source

142
00:07:12.394 --> 00:07:13.394
files.

143
00:07:14.444 --> 00:07:18.454
After the core loop feels natural, add
only the tools that solve

144
00:07:18.454 --> 00:07:21.211
a real problem. Layouts repeat useful
races.

145
00:07:21.211 --> 00:07:24.783
Named agents keep a role and project
memory across tasks.

146
00:07:24.783 --> 00:07:27.478
Schedules start ordinary runs on a
cadence.

147
00:07:27.478 --> 00:07:30.423
Routines record a shell workflow and
replay it.

148
00:07:30.423 --> 00:07:33.243
GitHub and Linear turn issues into
worktrees.

149
00:07:33.243 --> 00:07:37.065
The browser's design mode sends an exact
element to an agent.

150
00:07:37.065 --> 00:07:41.514
The command line, control surface, mobile
companion, secrets proxy, MCP

151
00:07:41.514 --> 00:07:45.462
gateway, and plugins are there when you
need deeper automation.

152
00:07:46.512 --> 00:07:49.483
Use small outcomes with visible acceptance
checks.

153
00:07:49.483 --> 00:07:52.513
Match the number of agents to the
decision's value.

154
00:07:52.513 --> 00:07:54.890
Never merge on a polished summary alone.

155
00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:56.851
Read the diff and run the checks.

156
00:07:56.851 --> 00:08:00.119
Review repository setup commands before
granting trust.

157
00:08:00.119 --> 00:08:03.387
Keep the control and companion servers
token-protected.

158
00:08:03.387 --> 00:08:07.249
Store durable context in notes, and use
the secrets proxy when an

159
00:08:07.249 --> 00:08:10.160
agent needs an API without seeing the
credential.

160
00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.418
The final merge remains your decision.

161
00:08:13.468 --> 00:08:15.498
Your first session is five moves.

162
00:08:15.498 --> 00:08:18.143
Open a repository. Write one small
outcome.

163
00:08:18.143 --> 00:08:20.912
Choose one verified agent and create the
run.

164
00:08:20.912 --> 00:08:22.573
Review its diff and checks.

165
00:08:22.573 --> 00:08:25.403
Then merge it only when the evidence is
clear.

166
00:08:25.403 --> 00:08:28.971
On the second task, try a duel and compare
two approaches.

167
00:08:28.971 --> 00:08:32.600
You can return to this video from Asylum's
Help menu at any

168
00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:35.307
time, with captions and the full
transcript.

169
00:08:35.307 --> 00:08:39.613
That is the whole mental model: separate
tables, visible evidence, one

170
00:08:39.613 --> 00:08:40.721
deliberate winner.
